title A tier is a cadence rather than an exemption -- the roster's every-lap set stays affordable and the choirs stay heard
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Opened cold and the cold roster earned its place immediately: sh tools/fixtures/standing_equipment_run.sh read 60 guards, 57 green, 3 RED on a tree whose previous lap had staged 37 paths and stopped. geode_libraries and readme_metrics compare a generated page against a fresh render and tools/hooks/pre-commit regenerates both at commit time, so a lap that never commits leaves both stale. readme_reach found three Markdown link targets in construction/archive/REDS-a-citation-in-a-comment-rows-218.md spelled ](../tools/...) from a directory two levels down -- the link TEXT beside each already read ../../tools/..., so the display was right and the target was wrong in all three.
think Standfast, so those closed before anything else. sed repointed the three targets, both writers regenerated their pages, and all three guards read green on metal individually before the full hot pass. Then git add -A and the hot roster over the tree the commit would ship: 60 of 60 green in 1,220 seconds. Shipped as ae54ca5e1e to origin and xykj61, which is where that round should have ended a lap earlier.
think The lap index is 3428 before that commit and 3429 after, so the council rota reads row 3429 mod 5 = 4 -- Earth and Mercury, the concrete: the one-clock naming law, Two Rooms, and the marked value. Two of the three governed this lap directly. Two Rooms says a claim crosses from the vision room to the checkable room by exactly one door, which is a witness -- and the previous lap had left the tier PROPOSED. The marked value says every value crossing a seam wears a mark naming its type, which is the tier field one layer down: a roster row crossing into the runner now wears a mark naming its cadence.
think Took the crux the previous recommend named, and approved it myself rather than leaving it proposed. It touches no custody gate -- no key, no signature, no provisioning, no force-push -- and the standing instruction is to approve my own design rounds. Said so plainly in the note rather than quietly.
think The design risk is the whole reason to be careful, and it is worth writing down. REDS %219's lesson is that an unheard choir is a refusal nobody receives. A tier field is one keystroke from being that same condition with a friendlier name: tier hand meaning runs when someone remembers would write the bug into a schema and call it a feature. So the shape is narrower -- a tier is a CADENCE rather than an exemption, every rostered guard runs on a clock, and the tier names which clock. Two words the tree already uses: lap, and cadence for the fifth round where the rota closes and the seed ships.
think Three things hold it honest, and each is a mechanism rather than an intention. guards_unknown_tier is gated at zero, so a guard seated at tier weekly is refused rather than run past -- it would otherwise run on no lap at all, in silence. cadence_never_run_here prints on its own line, because the every-lap tier announces its own absence by failing to run while a slower tier can go quiet with everything around it green. And witness_reach_scan.sh splits standing from cadence rather than blurring them.
think That third one is the subtle one and I nearly missed it. The reach meter read roster roots as one set. Seating the crypto choir at cadence would have moved 82 witnesses into standing -- a number reported as sung EVERY lap on the strength of a row that runs once in five. Truer-sounding than the number before it, and false. So the closure walk was factored into one closure() function called twice, from tier-lap roots plus hooks for standing and from tier-cadence roots for cadence, with the second minus the first. One walk written once, so the two readings cannot drift.
think Chose crypto_suite as the first cadence row rather than moving caravan_suite off the every-lap tier. caravan_suite had just held a live refusal for two laps and earns its seat; crypto reads GREEN today and costs 9m06s, which is exactly the case the tier exists for. The row also lowers unheard by one, since crypto_suite was itself among the five names traced by hand out of the 1,177 and nothing on disk invoked it.
think Made the scan stricter on one point it already claimed: the witness header said every guard record carries exactly ONE path, and the code enforced at-least-one, silently ignoring a second. Changed to -ne 1 and planted the two-path case in the control, rather than leaving a comment that overstates what runs.
think Wrote the runner control against a stub interpreter in a pen with no git repository, which proves two things at once -- which guards a pass SELECTS, independent of what any witness answers, and that the staged reading answers 0 outside a repository rather than refusing. A scan reading a file cannot prove a tier is honored; only driving the runner can.
obs The measurement. Cold roster 60 guards, 57 green, 3 red. Hot roster after repair 60 of 60 green in 1,220 seconds, which is the roster's first honestly timed full pass and the number the whole tier argument rests on. Reach before this round 1,690 / 167 standing / 513 sung / 1,177 unheard; after 1,690 / 167 standing / 82 cadence / 514 sung / 1,176 unheard, ceiling lowered to 1,176 with no slack. Roster 61 rows: 60 lap, 1 cadence.
obs Two counts of two different things, and both carry their denominator. The crypto choir sings 74 rungs in 9m06s, which is what a stopwatch measured last lap. The reach meter reads 82 witnesses entering the cadence set, which is the transitive closure of everything that row names minus what the every-lap tier already carried. Neither is the other's rounding, and writing one where the other belongs is how a share loses its denominator.
obs Booked REDS %220 for the git-add close firing a second time in two days. %188 concluded no guard can enforce it, which holds -- such a guard would have to run after a lap ends. That conclusion was wider than it needed to be about READING, and the distinction is the useful part: the runner now prints staged_uncommitted before the first guard, so this lap would have opened on staged_uncommitted=37 at line one rather than meeting the consequence eleven guards later. On a hot run the same number is the round's own work and means nothing.
obs The two findings are one finding, which is the part worth carrying forward. A close that costs twenty-five minutes is a close that gets skipped. Naming the reading is the cheap half; making the close affordable is the half that changes behaviour, and that is the tier.
obs Folded REDS %219 to construction/archive/REDS-a-choir-off-the-roster-rows-219.md as %220 carried the pin past 24,576, and deepened every root-relative link by one level in the same pass -- text AND target, which is the exact half the previous fold missed and readme_reach caught this morning. The ledger headline moved 219 to 220 and its remainder 199 to 200; reds_ledger_monotone GREEN.
obs Recorded checkpoint ae54ca5e1e before condensing roughly 1.2 KB of landed accounts off the operator card. The Now block carried it 939 bytes past its declared bound, so the comment-dial paragraph became a two-line pointer at the verdict document that holds every figure, and the Region-fold line left with its detail in its own log. Card reads 24,543 against 24,576.
obs The tier proved itself on metal in two passes rather than in one claim. A bare run read tier_run=lap, guards_run=60, 60 green in 1,339 seconds, with the cadence row correctly left alone. Then --tier cadence read guards_run=1, crypto_suite green in 520 seconds, and construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri now carries `ran crypto_suite 20260825.115340 green cadence` -- so cadence_never_run_here fell from 1 to 0, which is the number that tells a later lap whether the slower clock is actually being read.
obs Two false starts on the hot pass, both mine, both caught by the roster rather than by care. The first left `NIB_HERE` standing in the card's Git nib field and filed the %220 row at archive link depth inside the living pin. The second carried construction/ITINERARY.md to 24,718 bytes against the 24,576 it declares, because the measured tier readings went in after the trim that had brought it to 24,543. Both stopped at the guard that bit rather than run to the end, and the card reads 24,529 now.
obs Graded every artifact. The design note read B+ at 89 with a 38% negative share against Field's 30%, so it took a Gauge sweep -- eight sentences softened, every claim held exactly -- and reads A at 96 with 19%. Nothing below B, so no molt frame was pushed.
obs Wrote the %220 row at archive depth and filed it in the living pin, so three of its links pointed one level too deep and one dropped the archive/ prefix -- the same link-depth class this lap had already repaired in the %218 archive file six hours earlier. readme_reach bit it on the first hot pass, along with a `NIB_HERE` placeholder still standing in the card's Git nib field. Stopped that pass at guard fifty rather than letting it finish, since a run overlapping an edit measures neither tree, and restarted at guard one.
